Sorry for yet another question, I am getting ready to present my model internally and I have a question on the model presented in the GWAS-by-subtraction paper as I have seen similar values in my own model. I am just wondering why the path loading (lambda Cog-CP) is 0.4465 and not closer to 1.0. All the best, Joan

From u0b5254eeb9973d7a at groups.google.com  Thu Apr 30 18:48:20 2020
From: u0b5254eeb9973d7a at groups.google.com (Elliot Tucker-Drob)
Date: Thu, 30 Apr 2020 18:48:20 +0000
Subject: Re: GWAS-by-subtraction
Message-ID: <CWFYHd_X3mo.DVBbOh8QBwAJ@genomic-sem-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <CWFYHd_X3mo.mlf_ysgPBwAJ@genomic-sem-users.groups.google.com>
References: <CWFYHd_X3mo.mlf_ysgPBwAJ@genomic-sem-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/genomic-sem-users/c/CWFYHd_X3mo/m/DVBbOh8QBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The standardized loading of Cog Performance on Cog will be 1.0 as the residual variance of Cog Performance is constrained to 0. Since the Cog factor is scaled to have a variance of 1.0 (such that it is on a Z scale), the unstandardized loading of the Cog Performance variable on the Cog factor is a scaling constant. Squaring the unstandardized loading will give the SNP heritability.

